: 10091.4, "nodata_rule": "a sample is no-data, and never enters a statistic, when its band is missing or NaN, equals the model's nodata_value, lies outside the band's declared regression range, or carries no class on a classification band"}}, "seq": 13, "t": 148.171, "seconds": 81.694, "call_seq": 12}
{"type": "final", "turn": 5, "content": "\n\nDone \u2014 compared both 2025 maps over the PA Karst area (shared extent ~10,091 km\u00b2, 6x6 window grid, 25 valid samples; 11 no-data dropped).\n\n**Headline:** the two maps do not agree at all \u2014 Pearson correlation **-0.017** across sampled windows, i.e. zero relationship. They are near-independent predictions of the same landscape.\n\n| | KarstBinary | KarstNumber |\n|---|---|---|\n| result_id | `419c581d-\u2026f26b` | `afb1cf68-\u20269ae8` |\n| band | `sample_karst_score` | `sample_number` |\n| range | 0\u20131 (score) | 0.2\u20131.2 (count-like value) |\n| mean over sampled windows | 0.10 | 0.68 |\n\nCaveats:\n- The bands are **different quantities**, so only the correlation is meaningful; the mean gap (~0.58), RMSE (0.62) and 0% within-tolerance agreement must not be read as \"one is higher/better.\"\n- Sampled on a coarse grid \u2014 an estimate of where they differ, not every pixel. Practically: everywhere is a disagreement zone here, so if you need spatial detail, zoom into the tile layers side-by-side in Studio/QGIS (I can build a QGIS layer pack for either map).\n\n**Which one is right?** I can't answer that honestly from these tools alone: no ground-truth labels exist for this AOI, and without labels model-vs-model comparison reports agreement only, never accuracy (the more confident side wins only ~51\u201370% of contested windows). To settle it I'd need labeled karst samples \u2014 e.g., run a label-sample design on one (or both) results, label those windows, then compute per-map error rates. Say the word and I'll set up the sampling plan.", "seq": 14, "t": 159.185, "seconds": 11.014}
